„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_and that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book1§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢Wherefore, my beloved, as ye have always obeyed, not as in my presence only, but now much more in my absence, work out your own salvation with fear and trembling.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOFor it is God which worketh in you both to will and to do of his good pleasure.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book1§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ0Do all things without murmurings and disputings:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢that ye may be blameless and harmless, the sons of God, without rebuke, in the midst of a crooked and perverse nation, among whom ye shine as lights in the world;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙholding forth the word of life; that I may rejoice in the day of Christ, that I have not run in vain, neither laboured in vain.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book1§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgYea, and if I be offered upon the sacrifice and service of your faith, I joy, and rejoice with you all.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ7For the same cause also do ye joy, and rejoice with me.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book1§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}But I trust in the Lord Jesus to send Timotheus shortly unto you, that I also may be of good comfort, when I know your state.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙEFor I have no man likeminded, who will naturally care for your state.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@For all seek their own, not the things which are Jesus Christ's.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙcBut ye know the proof of him, that, as a son with the father, he hath served with me in the gospel.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVHim therefore I hope to send presently, so soon as I shall see how it will go with me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>But I trust in the Lord that I also myself shall come shortly.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book1§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨Yet I supposed it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother, and companion in labour, and fellowsoldier, but your messenger, and he that ministered to my wants.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhFor he longed after you all, and was full of heaviness, because that ye had heard that he had been sick.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFor indeed he was sick nigh unto death: but God had mercy on him; and not on him only, but on me also, lest I should have sorrow upon sorrow.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ{I sent him therefore the more carefully, that, when ye see him again, ye may rejoice, and that I may be the less sorrowful.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQReceive him therefore in the Lord with all gladness; and hold such in reputation:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxbecause for the work of Christ he was nigh unto death, not regarding his life, to supply your lack of service toward me.¦layout ªlast_verse